===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|composition}} A musical [[composition]] with [[lyrics]] for [[voice]] or voices, performed by [[singing]].
#: {{ux|en|Thomas listened to his favorite '''song''' on the radio yesterday.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Cornishe [''i.e.'', {{w|William Cornysh}}]|chapter=In the Fleete Made by Me William Cornishe otherwise Called Nyshwhete Chapelman with the Most Famose and Noble Kyng [[w:Henry VII of England|Henry the VII.]] His Reygne the XIX. Yere the Moneth of July. A Treatise betwene Trouth, and Information.|mainauthor=w:John Skelton|editor=[[w:John Stow|J[ohn] S[tow]]]|title=Pithy Pleasaunt and Profitable Workes of Maister Skelton, Poete Laureate|location=Imprinted at London: In [[w:Fleet Street|Fletestreate]], neare vnto [[w:St Dunstan-in-the-West|Saint Dunstones Churche]] by Thomas Marshe|year=1568|oclc=54747393|title2=Pithy Pleasaunt and Profitable Workes of Maister Skelton, Poete Laureate to King Henry the VIIIth|location2=London|publisher2=Printed for C. Davis in [[w:Paternoster Row|Pater-noster Row]]|year2=1736|page2=290|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ry8JAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA290|oclc2=731569711|passage=''The Harpe.'' {{...}} A harper with his [[wrest#Noun|wreſt]] maye tune the harpe wrong / Mys tunying of an Inſtrument ſhal hurt a true '''ſonge'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|author=Mrs M.A. Thompson|chapter=The Tutor's Daughter|title=Graham's American Monthly Magazine of Literature, Art, and Fashion|page=[http://books.google.com/books?id=hdcRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA266 266]
|passage=In the lightness of my heart I sang catches of '''songs''' as my horse gayly bore me along the well-remembered road.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=5|passage=He was thinking; but the glory of the '''song''', the swell from the great organ, the clustered lights,{{nb...}}, the height and vastness of this noble fane, its antiquity and its strength—all these things seemed to have their part as causes of the thrilling emotion that accompanied his thoughts.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any musical [[composition]].
# Poetical composition; [[poetry]]; [[verse]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=This subject for heroic '''song'''.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|passage=The bard that first adorned our native tongue / Tuned to his British lyre this ancient '''song'''.}}
# The act or [[art]] of [[sing]]ing.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Spencer Leigh Hughes|title=The Weather. A Short Study on a Great Subject.|journal=Golden Hours: A Monthly Magazine for Family and General Reading|volume=XVII|location=London|publisher=Lile and Fawcett,{{nb...|Ludgate Circus and Salisbury Square, E.C.}}|year=1884|page=28|column=1|passage=How often the enthusiast has dwelt upon the birds bursting into '''song''', the buds bursting into flower, all nature bursting into life!—as though a state of things in which everything around us is bursting is at all pleasant.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|url=https://books.google.com.tr/books?id=8ltZAAAAMAAJ&q=%22+one+that+%22subject:%22poetry%22&dq=%22+one+that+%22subject:%22poetry%22|title=The Substance that is Poetry|author=Robert Peter Tristram Coffin|publisher=Macmillan|series=Patten Foundation series|lccn=42022107|oclc=1072148420|year=1942|page=71|passage=Or take one that is less of an explanation and more of a '''song''' , The Spider . I knew all along what I wanted to say about a spider . I wanted to say all the good things I could . For spiders are the one order of creation that I  thoroughly dislike.{{...}}}}
# A [[melodious]] sound made by a [[bird]], [[insect]], [[whale]] or other [[animal]].
#: {{ux|en|I love hearing the '''song''' of canary birds.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1833|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=The Canterbury Pilgrims
|passage=That most ethereal of all sounds, the '''song''' of crickets.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=85|passage=The robin alone by his soft morning '''song''' broke the silence and the solitude which reigned in the forest.}}
# {{lb|en|ornithology}} The distinctive sound that a male bird utters to attract a [[mate]] or to [[protect]] his [[territory]]; {{n-g|contrasts with [[call]]}}; also, similar vocalisations made by female birds.
# A low [[price]], especially one under the expected [[value]]; chiefly in {{m|en|for a song}}.
#: {{ux|en|He bought that car for a '''song'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1810|author=w:Benjamin Silliman|title=A Journal of Travels in England, Holland and Scotland
|passage=his [a common soldier's] pay is a '''song'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Belloc Lowndes Lodger|I|0016
|passage=Thus the red damask curtains which now shut out the fog-laden, drizzling atmosphere of the Marylebone Road, had cost a mere '''song''', and yet they might have been warranted to last another thirty years. A great bargain also had been the excellent Axminster carpet which covered the floor;{{nb...}}.}}
# An object of [[derision]]; a [[laughing stock]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Job|30|9|passage=And now am I their '''song''', yea, I am their byword.}}

